{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2604.17931","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2604.17931","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2604.17931","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bince","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Bince","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanli","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wanli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LiteResearcher: A Scalable Agentic RL Training Framework for Deep Research Agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-04-20T08:11:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:50:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-22T09:13:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-23T00:40:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T17:17:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-01T01:16:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-01T16:44:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-02T01:03:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-26T12:18:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-28T00:52:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning (RL) has emerged as a powerful training paradigm for LLM-based agents. However, scaling agentic RL for deep research remains constrained by two coupled challenges: hand-crafted synthetic data fails to elicit genuine real-world search capabilities, and real-world search dependency during RL training introduces instability and prohibitive cost, which limits the scalability of Agentic RL. LiteResearcher is a training framework that makes Agentic RL scalable: by constructing a lite virtual world that mirrors real-world search dynamics, we enable a continuously improving training recipe that empowers a tiny search agent to outperform large-scale open-source and commercial models (e.g., Tongyi DeepResearch and Claude-4.5 Sonnet). Specifically, on common benchmarks such as GAIA and Xbench, our LiteResearcher-4B achieves open-source state-of-the-art results of 71.3% and 78.0% respectively, demonstrating that scalable RL training is a key enabler for Deep Research Agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"COLM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.17931","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-04-21T04:22:47Z","registered":"2026-04-21T04:22:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:58:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2604.17896","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2604.17896","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2604.17896","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yubai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hashem","familyName":"Haghbayan","name":"Haghbayan, Hashem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Explicit Physical Feasibility Benefit VLA Learning? An Empirical Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-04-20T07:15:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:46:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-05T10:16:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-06T00:37:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models map multimodal inputs directly to robot actions and are typically trained through large-scale imitation learning. While this paradigm has shown strong performance, prevailing VLA training procedures do not explicitly supervise hard physical constraints such as obstacle avoidance or kinematic feasibility. As a result, the geometric structure underlying physically feasible behavior must be inferred only implicitly from demonstrations. In this paper, we study whether introducing explicit feasibility supervision can provide effective structured guidance for VLA policies. We formulate a simple geometry-grounded feasibility objective and integrate it into the training stage of a diffusion-based VLA policy. To evaluate this idea systematically, we use obstacle-aware manipulation as a controlled probe of geometry-dependent physical feasibility. Empirical results show that augmenting VLA training with feasibility supervision improves both physical reliability and overall task performance, while also enhancing learning efficiency in the low-data regime. These findings indicate that explicit feasibility signals can effectively complement imitation-based VLA learning, highlighting their potential for developing more reliable VLA policies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 5 figures. This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.17896","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-04-21T04:21:59Z","registered":"2026-04-21T04:22:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-06T02:19:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2604.17821","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2604.17821","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2604.17821","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yongan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinpeng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jinpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Ying","name":"Ying, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuien","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kuien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zenglin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zenglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WebUncertainty: Dual-Level Uncertainty Driven Planning and Reasoning For Autonomous Web Agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-04-20T05:19:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:41:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T03:15:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:28:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have empowered autonomous web agents to execute natural language instructions directly on real-world webpages. However, existing agents often struggle with complex tasks involving dynamic interactions and long-horizon execution due to rigid planning strategies and hallucination-prone reasoning. To address these limitations, we propose WebUncertainty, a novel autonomous agent framework designed to tackle dual-level uncertainty in planning and reasoning. Specifically, we design a Task Uncertainty-Driven Adaptive Planning Mechanism that adaptively selects planning modes to navigate unknown environments. Furthermore, we introduce an Action Uncertainty-Driven Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS) Reasoning Mechanism. This mechanism incorporates the Confidence-induced Action Uncertainty (ConActU) strategy to quantify both aleatoric uncertainty (AU) and epistemic uncertainty (EU), thereby optimizing the search process and guiding robust decision-making. Experimental results on the WebArena and WebVoyager benchmarks demonstrate that WebUncertainty achieves superior performance compared to state-of-the-art baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.17821","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-04-21T04:20:12Z","registered":"2026-04-21T04:20:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:37:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":3,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=(doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2604.17821%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2604.17896%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2604.17931)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=3"}}